  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Monday)\nIssued at 1236 PM CDT TUE JUL 21 2026\n\nWhile Tropical Storm Bertha remains the most notable weather\nsystem near the state, it will actually have the least amount of\nimpact across the region over the next seven days. TS Bertha will\ncontinue to slowly trek to the NW today, before taking a more\nwestern track come Wednesday morning. During this time, only\nscattered shower activity is expected across the region, as the\nouter bands from TS Bertha work inland. During this same time, a\ndeep trough and cold front will slowly start dropping south\nthrough the Great Lakes, working into the region sometime\nWednesday night into Thursday morning. This front will quickly\nbecome separated from the main trough to the north, stalling\nsomewhere across the state into the weekend. Not only will this\ncold front cool things down a few degrees, but it will also be\nthe catalyst for more organized rain activity heading into the\nweekend.\n\nUntil this trough and cold front break this hot streak we've been\nexperiencing, afternoon highs today and tomorrow will continue to\nrange in the low to mid-90s. Given the high humidity, this will\nkeep heat index values above 105 degrees for many locations, with\na Heat Advisory remaining in effect for today. The main axis of\nheat seems to shift slightly Southwest into Wednesday, so a new\nHeat Advisory will go into effect Wednesday morning to better\ncapture this shift in heat index values. Unfortunately, the break\nin temperatures this weekend appears short-lived, as afternoon \nhighs climb once more by the start of the new workweek under a \nbuilding ridge to our west.\n\n/44/",
